<!--
    kuna strict fix (no option): SPARC returns a long long in %o0:%o1 with the
    HIGH word in %o0, and returns an int in %o0 alone.

    The same join-order bug as kuna-bejoin.xml (the PowerPC case): return
    recovery joined %o0 as the LOW word, so wide_mul printed
    CONCAT44(low,high).  SPARC adds a second shape.  `restore` copies every
    in-register back to its out-register, so %o1 reaches the RETURN holding
    the incoming second argument, and both registers look returned.  The
    uncomputed-half repair used to drop that %o1 only by accident of the wrong
    order.  With the order fixed, a %o1 the window hands back holding its entry
    value, or a literal the function also used for something else, is a
    leftover, and the function returns %o0 alone.  Before the fix:

        wide_mul:  return CONCAT44((int4)((int8)a1 * a0),... >> 0x20);   (halves swapped)
        same:      if (a0 == a1) return 1; return (uint8)a1 << 0x20;    (returns a1 << 32)
        keep_zero: *a0 = a1; return (uint8)a1 << 0x20;                 (returns a1 << 32)

    Stage: P4 calls and prototypes (output-prototype).  No option: this only
    corrects wrong output, and every little-endian target is byte-identical.

    Source (clang 14 -O2, sparc-linux-gnu, bytes from bejoin_sparc32_be.o):
        long long wide_mul(int a, int b) { return (long long)a * b; }
          save / smul %i1,%i0,%i1 / rd %y,%i0 / ret / restore
        unsigned int same(unsigned int a, unsigned int b) { return a == b; }
          save / cmp %i0,%i1 / be / ret / restore %g0,%g0,%o0 / ret / restore %g0,1,%o0
        int keep_zero(int *p, int b) { *p = b; return 0; }
          save / mov %g0,%i2 / st %i1,[%i0] / ret / restore %g0,%i2,%o0

    The window hands back whatever %i1 holds, not only the argument.  From
    bejoin_window.c (clang 14, sparc-linux-gnu, bytes from
    bejoin_window_sparc32_O2.o at 0x20000 and bejoin_window_sparc32_O0.o's
    mark at 0x30000), four functions that return one int.  Before:

        back4 -O2:      return (uint8)((int4)v1[-3] + 100) << 0x20;   (%i1 is the zero byte the loop exits on)
        zero_after -O2: sub_20080(a1,a0); return a1;                   (the literal 0 folded away)
        one_after -O2:  if (1 <= a0) return 1; return a1;             (same, on one path)
        mark -O0:       return (uint8)(a1 + 1) << 0x20;              (mov %g0,%i1 zeroes a byte store)

    zero_after and one_after also pass the argument to a call, whose pcode
    keeps a RETURN for a delay-slot restore that the constant didrestore = 0
    never reaches.

    Assert #1: wide_mul is the signed 64-bit product, rd %y (the high word) in %o0.
    Asserts #2-#3, #5: same and keep_zero return one int in %o0, and return 0.
    Assert #4: no function returns the second argument shifted into the high word.
    Asserts #6-#9: back4, mark, zero_after and one_after return one int in %o0,
    never the second argument, and nothing is shifted into the high word.
-->
